\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{What the first theorem programme must distinguish}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{scaling-law}
\tag{research-programme}

\p{The definition in \ref{ftip-000D} makes potential conditional on the base
artifact, task law, inference budget, lifecycle budget, protocol class, and
trust contract. This is the smallest shape broad enough to compare pretraining
efficiency, reward design, agentic sandbox cost, rollout reuse, and test-time
search without treating them as one scalar resource.}

\p{The literature now suggests five first theorem lanes:}

\ol{
  \li{\strong{Information and coverage}: lower bounds from verifier noise,
  preference information, missing support, and distribution shift.}
  \li{\strong{Reward and credit}: conditional guarantees and reward-hacking
  counterexamples for outcome, process, preference, and latent targets.}
  \li{\strong{Support and entropy}: finite rollout laws, acquisition witnesses,
  and separations between global entropy, token entropy, surprisal, and
  effective successful support.}
  \li{\strong{Update stability and trajectory geometry}: safe reuse bounds,
  gradient-alarm limitations, path-emulation regret, and no-free-feedback
  results inspired by DGG and NExt.}
  \li{\strong{Compute allocation}: optimal trade-offs among fresh rollouts,
  verifier work, updates, environments, checkpoint prediction, and deployment
  inference under explicitly diminishing returns.}
}

\p{Architecture and pretraining enter these theorems through named conditions:
base support, representation or Jacobian geometry, routing stability, optimizer
efficiency, and per-token or per-environment cost. They should affect rates or
ceilings only where a proof or falsifier establishes the dependence.}

\p{This first draft does not choose the final theorem statements. It fixes the
storyline and semantic interfaces from which those statements can be asked.
Follow-up drafts should add finite examples, assumption tables, and theorem
cards only after each observable and cost has been tied to a literature-backed
measurement protocol.}
